From dd83ae297ec9d52410c6d672816e9147145a5066 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:24:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision through the converter of an
 adapter or a USB-C hub too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The kernel now asks the port's DPCD which DisplayPort to HDMI converter
is behind it (linux-9890), so DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER is on every
DisplayPort connector of i915 and xe, and a converter that is not on the
board (an adapter, the HDMI socket of a USB-C hub, as on a Chromebook)
is "unlisted": offered where the user sets "Dolby Vision through a
display converter" to On, as with a board that is not on the list.

- The setting is shown where a converter that the driver takes is
  behind the port (DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER is not "none"), not wherever
  the connector has the property: most DisplayPort outputs have a
  DisplayPort display or nothing behind them, and there it would do
  nothing. Its help and the reasons of the Dolby Vision page say "a
  display converter", not "the board's", and name adapters and USB-C
  hubs (English, and the translations of es_es, es_ar and es_mx).
- The lines of kodi.log say where the converter may be and no longer
  call it the board's.

The idea of Dolby Vision through a USB-C hub on a Chromebook is from PR
#29 of LibreELEC Hades by Felix Oghină.
