 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#41301"
-msgid "Some boards have a DisplayPort to HDMI converter between the graphics port and the HDMI socket (many mini PCs and NUCs with Intel graphics of the 11th generation and later), which Linux treats as a DisplayPort output. A converter turns what the graphics send into HDMI in its own way, and one that does not carry the Dolby Vision signal leaves the TV without Dolby Vision, or without a picture. Automatic offers Dolby Vision through it only on the boards where it was seen working. On offers it through any converter that the driver takes: try it if your TV shows no Dolby Vision from the HDMI socket of your board, and set it back if the picture goes. Off never uses it. It is not used on a native HDMI output. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "Some boards have a DisplayPort to HDMI converter between the graphics port and the HDMI socket (many mini PCs and NUCs with Intel graphics of the 11th generation and later), and USB-C adapters and hubs with an HDMI socket have one too (that is how a Chromebook reaches a TV). Linux treats such an output as a DisplayPort one. A converter turns what the graphics send into HDMI in its own way, and one that does not carry the Dolby Vision signal leaves the TV without Dolby Vision, or without a picture. Automatic offers Dolby Vision through it only where it was seen working. On offers it through any converter that the driver takes: try it if your TV shows no Dolby Vision through the HDMI socket of your board or of your adapter, and set it back if the picture goes. Off never uses it. It is not used on a native HDMI output. Applies from the next video."
